---
import ArticleLayout from "../../layouts/ArticleLayout.astro";

const description =
  "Why a well-maintained edge requires less force, plus practical habits for safer, more controlled cutting.";
---

<ArticleLayout title="The Golden Rules for a Safer Kitchen" description={description} category="Kitchen safety">
  <p class="article-lead">
    A dull knife often needs extra pressure and is more likely to snag or slip. A sharp knife still deserves complete respect, but its clean, predictable cut can give you better control.
  </p>

  <div class="article-warning">
    <h2>A sharp knife is still a sharp knife.</h2>
    <p>Good edge maintenance reduces the force needed to cut; careful technique and attention are what make that advantage safer.</p>
  </div>

  <section>
    <h2>Why dull knives can be unpredictable</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>More pressure:</strong> forcing a blade through food makes it harder to stop or correct if something shifts.</li>
      <li><strong>Uneven cuts:</strong> a dull edge tears and crushes instead of moving cleanly through the food.</li>
      <li><strong>More slipping:</strong> an edge that cannot bite into a smooth surface may skid before it starts cutting.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>What a maintained edge improves</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Precision and control:</strong> less force makes small adjustments easier.</li>
      <li><strong>Cleaner contact:</strong> the blade is more likely to start where you place it.</li>
      <li><strong>Less fatigue:</strong> efficient cutting is easier on your hands during longer prep sessions.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Seven habits for safer cutting</h2>
    <div class="safety-grid">
      <article><span aria-hidden="true">01</span><h3>Maintain the edge</h3><p>Hone and sharpen as needed so the blade cuts without excessive force.</p></article>
      <article><span aria-hidden="true">02</span><h3>Use a secure grip</h3><p>Hold the handle firmly, keep it dry, and keep fingers away from the blade’s path.</p></article>
      <article><span aria-hidden="true">03</span><h3>Stabilize the board</h3><p>Use a non-slip board or place a damp towel underneath it.</p></article>
      <article><span aria-hidden="true">04</span><h3>Stay focused</h3><p>Avoid distractions and never rush when a knife is in your hand.</p></article>
      <article><span aria-hidden="true">05</span><h3>Protect your guide hand</h3><p>Use a claw grip and consider a cut-resistant glove when appropriate.</p></article>
      <article><span aria-hidden="true">06</span><h3>Store knives safely</h3><p>Use a block, magnetic strip, or blade guards instead of a loose drawer.</p></article>
      <article><span aria-hidden="true">07</span><h3>Learn the motion</h3><p>Practice controlled slicing and keep the blade moving away from your body.</p></article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Respect the tool</h2>
    <p>
      Knife safety comes from a maintained edge, a stable workspace, sound technique, and your full attention. Keep those pieces together and every prep session becomes more controlled and enjoyable.
    </p>
  </section>
</ArticleLayout>
